Downstream ROS handles kPause/kCancel/kRetry explicitly; kNone falls through (no-op). So pushing a kNone action is safe — downstream ignores it. That satisfies "不 pause 活跃任务" while keeping the finding visible in the actions list. 

Hmm, but should I push kNone actions at all? Two design choices:
- A: push action with kind=kNone (visible, downstream no-ops).
- B: skip pushing entirely for kOverCapacity.

The plan says "转为暂缓派发/hold 类动作，复用现有动作枚举中最贴近者" — "convert to a dispatch-hold-like action, reusing the closest existing action enum". This implies we DO emit an action, of the closest kind — which is kNone (the only non-pause/cancel/retry option). So option A: emit kNone.

